From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 10:12:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] A seek forgets that the reader passed the play item's OUT
 time

When the PCR of a clip passes the OUT time of its play item, the filter
tells the reader, and the reader skips the rest of the clip at its next
aligned unit (out_hit, "Skip the rest of a clip once PCR has passed the
play item OUT time"). A seek within the clip starts the filter again
(m2ts_filter_seek()), but left out_hit as it was. Where the seek came
between the read of the unit in which the PCR passed OUT and the next
read, the first read after the seek skipped the clip from the seek's
target to its end: the title ended there, or the next play item began,
instead of the play item going on from the target.

_seek_stream() now clears out_hit with the state of the filter, as
_open_m2ts() does for a new clip.

Checked on a disc tree made for the test (one clip, a play item whose
OUT time is before the end of its clip): for every number of reads of
192 bytes, and of 6144 bytes, before a seek to the start of the play
item or to its second mark, the reads after the seek give the same bytes
as the same seek made at once. Before, a seek right after the read of
that unit gave nothing (32 of 162 with reads of 192 bytes, 1 of 5 with
reads of 6144 bytes).
---
 src/libbluray/bluray.c | 3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/libbluray/bluray.c b/src/libbluray/bluray.c
index 0872a3d..35cdb38 100644
--- a/src/libbluray/bluray.c
+++ b/src/libbluray/bluray.c
@@ -1091,6 +1091,9 @@ static int64_t _seek_stream(BLURAY *bd, BD_STREAM *st,
     if (st->m2ts_filter) {
         m2ts_filter_seek(st->m2ts_filter, 0, (int64_t)st->clip->in_time << 1);
     }
+    /* the filter forgets the PCR it saw before the seek, and so does the reader: a PCR past the OUT time
+       seen in the unit read last must not skip the clip from the seek's target on */
+    st->out_hit = 0;
 
     st->clip_pos = (uint64_t)clip_pkt * 192;
     st->clip_block_pos = (st->clip_pos / 6144) * 6144;
